Z||000003||sjp777||12-06-2001||10:12 PM||||Your idea about having them entirly coated and then turned is the better option, if you can get them turned only in the area where the pad hits<P>The pad is about a 1/4" smaller than the rotor surface on each side. I bought new rotors, painted the hubs to get rid of that lovely rust look, and a few weeks later have a simply peachy 1/4" band of rust on the inner and outer tracks of the rotor where the pad don't hit.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg">     <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||12-06-2001||11:48 PM||||Um. Powder coating MELTS at about 400F!<P>Infact that's how it's applied (baked in an ordinary oven at 400 degrees for a day)<P><BR>Get the rotors Cadmium Plated instead.<P>This is like galvinising, but the layer of material applied is almost non-existant in terms of thickness.  It will NOT distort machined surfaces signifigantly either (+-0.0001)<P>You just assemble everything, and eventually the pads wear through the cad plated area, but everything else stays plated.<P>Works a jolly treat!<P>PS: you can do the whole suspension (knuckles, hubs, etc) too. Only things I would not do are actual bearing races/surfaces.<P>Best!<BR>Ben<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
